WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.200
"How Effective Are Statins?"

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:09.360
In my video on how doctors

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:11.840
paternalistically
mislead patients

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:15.440
about statin risks and benefits
for their own good,

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:16.400
I talk about how,

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:19.040
for the majority
of patients surveyed,

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:22.400
the expectation of benefit
from drugs like statins

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:26.080
is higher than the actual
benefit the drugs provide;

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:28.480
so, this creates tension

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:30.480
between a patient’s
right to know

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:32.880
and the likely reduction
in the chance

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:35.040
they’d agree to take it
if they knew

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:37.440
how little benefit
the drug offered.

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:40.960
On a population scale,
that would be devastating.

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:42.960
For example,
statins probably prevent

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:46.480
tens of thousands of deaths
a year in the United States.

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:50.400
However, if patients were
routinely told the truth,

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:53.600
as many as 75% of patients
might stop treatment.

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:56.400
So, unless we keep everyone
in the dark,

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:59.040
30,000 people could die.

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:01.760
So, what should doctors do?

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:05.680
I agree with these doctors
that we have to tell the truth

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:08.400
even if it means
the patient doesn’t take it

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:10.240
and potentially dies
as a result.

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:12.400
It’s their body, their choice.

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:15.200
Of course, I wish
this fully-informed consent

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:17.760
would extend to telling people
about the beneficial role

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:20.240
of a healthy diet,

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:22.320
but before we get into that,

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:24.080
let’s answer
the statins question.

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:25.760
Are they worth it?

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:27.520
Maybe you’re in the 25%
of people

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:31.120
who would still take it,
even knowing the whole truth.

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:34.080
So, what is the whole truth?

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:35.840
We’ve all seen the drug ads,

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:38.880
like this one touting
atorvastatin, Lipitor,

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:40.640
as reducing the risk
of a heart attack

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:42.240
by about a third.

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:44.400
But as you can see
in the small print,

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:46.560
"That means
in a large clinical study,

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:48.400
three percent of patients
taking a sugar pill

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:49.440
had a heart attack

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:52.480
compared to 2% of patients
taking Lipitor."

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:55.040
Going from three to two
is indeed a drop

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:57.920
by a third in relative risk,

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:02.000
but the drop in absolute risk
was only 1%,

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:04.400
which sounds less impressive.

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:06.880
This is common
even in the medical literature-

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:08.640
no surprise,
since journal articles

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:11.600
are often written
by drug manufacturers.

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:14.400
Now, the 3% to 2% Lipitor drop

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:16.560
was over a period
of only about three years.

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:19.200
These drugs are to be taken
over a lifetime;

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:21.440
so, the benefits accrue.

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:24.000
Over four or five years,
the absolute risk reduction

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:28.000
might reach 1.3%,
but you can see why

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:30.480
many patients
are not very moved.

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:32.400
Even in the studies
where people were presented

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:35.600
with an idealized tablet
with no side effects,

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:38.880
more than a third stated
that they would not consider

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:40.640
taking a medication
that would drop

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:44.880
their five-year absolute risk
by 5% or more.

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:47.280
Only about 50% of people
would consider

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:50.080
taking preventive medications
that prolonged their life

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:52.160
by less than eight months.

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:55.120
The average expected
longevity benefit from statins

00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:57.360
ranges from a few months
to a few years

00:02:57.360 --> 00:02:58.880
depending on risk.

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:00.640
In this patient population here,

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:04.240
the expected lifespan gain
would be about a year,

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:05.600
whereas here’s the distribution

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:08.800
of what people would want
to take a drug every day

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:10.080
for the rest of their lives.

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:12.640
About a third said,
"Three months?

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:13.840
I’ll take it."

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:17.040
Whereas about 1 in 10 said
they wouldn’t take pills

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:20.400
even if they got to live
an extra ten years or more.

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:23.920
That’s why
it’s such a personal decision,

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:25.760
because we are
all over the place

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:28.080
in terms of what
we’re willing to accept.

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:33.600
Your doctor may not care,
though.

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:36.160
Physicians were presented
with the case of a man

00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:39.360
with a 7% to 10% risk of dying
from cardiovascular disease

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:40.800
over the next decade

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:43.600
who explicitly told the doctor

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:46.000
that they only want
to take a statin

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:47.600
if it would increase
their lifespan

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:49.120
by a certain amount.

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:51.520
Some doctors were told
the patient demanded

00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:53.440
at least eight years of life,

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:55.200
and others were told
the patient only asked

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:56.560
for a matter of months,

00:03:56.560 --> 00:03:59.600
which is actually
what they’d gain in real life;

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:01.840
yet, 83% of the doctors said

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:03.360
they’d prescribe it
to the patients

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:06.160
who unrealistically demanded
eight years,

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:09.040
which is almost exactly
the same percentage

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:10.400
recommended by the doctors
who were told

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:12.720
the patient only wanted
a few months.

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:15.280
In other words,
the doctors were insensitive

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:19.680
to patient preferences
regarding survival gain.

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:21.760
Now, for primary prevention,

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:24.000
trying to prevent
your first heart attack,

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:26.800
no overall survival benefits
have been found.

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:29.680
Indeed, only one
of eight studies

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:32.480
showed that statins actually
made people live longer,

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:34.640
but presumably
that’s because the studies

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:38.080
only lasted a few years,
and for low-risk populations,

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:40.320
the risk of dying
from cardiovascular disease

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:44.560
in that time may just be
a few percent anyway.

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:46.800
But such trials do show
fewer events,

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:47.840
heart attacks and strokes;

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:50.240
so, on a public health scale,
it would make sense

00:04:50.240 --> 00:04:52.320
not to just wait for people
to have a heart attack

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:56.000
before starting them
on cholesterol-lowering drugs.

00:04:56.000 --> 00:04:59.520
Critics counter that, yes,
that sounds good,

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:01.840
and if statins had
these benefits

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:03.440
without side effects,

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:06.320
then perhaps the decision
to start them is understandable,

00:05:06.320 --> 00:05:11.120
but look, because statins
increase our risk of diabetes,

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:14.000
these drugs might give
as many people diabetes

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:15.280
as it does prevent them

00:05:15.280 --> 00:05:17.600
from having a heart attack
or stroke.

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:20.240
But the stat they cite
is from a combination

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:23.840
of primary and secondary
prevention trials.

00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:26.160
In primary prevention trials,

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:28.160
like trying to prevent
your first heart attack,

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:30.640
there is no increased
diabetes risk.

00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:33.200
You only see that
in secondary prevention trials

00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:34.320
where people are trying
to prevent

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:37.200
their second heart attack,
for instance.

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:40.400
This might be
because their risk of diabetes

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:41.760
is higher in general,

00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:44.480
or they’re using
higher doses of statins.

00:05:44.480 --> 00:05:46.240
Intensive-dose statin therapy

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:48.320
is associated with a greater
increased risk

00:05:48.320 --> 00:05:50.080
of new-onset diabetes

00:05:50.080 --> 00:05:52.720
compared to moderate-dose
statin therapy.

00:05:52.720 --> 00:05:55.920
Of course, intensive therapy
also offers more benefits

00:05:55.920 --> 00:05:58.800
in terms of cardiovascular
protection.

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:01.360
Then, if you separate out
the primary prevention trials

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:04.080
by the populations
who have low or high rates

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:06.640
of diabetes
regardless of the drug,

00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:09.360
statins only seem
to increase risk among those

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:11.840
with high baseline rates.

00:06:11.840 --> 00:06:14.080
Again, this could be
a consequence of running

00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:16.880
shorter-term trials
in low-risk populations.

00:06:16.880 --> 00:06:18.800
The big benefits,
preventing death,

00:06:18.800 --> 00:06:19.760
may not show up,

00:06:19.760 --> 00:06:21.040
but the big downside,

00:06:21.040 --> 00:06:22.640
like the drug
giving you diabetes,

00:06:22.640 --> 00:06:24.720
may also not show up.

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:27.760
But even in the trials that
do show increased diabetes,

00:06:27.760 --> 00:06:31.920
the risk of getting diabetes
may be vastly offset

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:36.320
by the cardiovascular protection
offered from statin therapy.

00:06:36.320 --> 00:06:38.000
Of course, a healthy diet

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:41.360
may reduce the risk of both
at the same time,

00:06:41.360 --> 00:06:43.280
but sticking with
the drug discussion

00:06:43.280 --> 00:06:44.720
before moving on to diet,

00:06:44.720 --> 00:06:46.640
increased diabetes risk

00:06:46.640 --> 00:06:48.560
is not the only side effect
of statins.

00:06:48.560 --> 00:06:52.480
What about risks to our muscles,
liver, kidney, and brain?

00:06:52.480 --> 00:06:56.910
I’ll get into
all of those, next.

